REFERENCE   2  (bases 1 to 1192)
  AUTHORS   Bertz,M., Kuhn,K., Koeberle,S.C., Muller,M.F., Hoelzer,D.,
            Thies,K., Deubel,S., Thierbach,R. and Kipp,A.P.
  TITLE     Selenoprotein H controls cell cycle progression and proliferation
            of human colorectal cancer cells
  JOURNAL   Free Radic Biol Med 127, 98-107 (2018)
   PUBMED   29330096
  REMARK    GeneRIF: The high levels of SELENOH in tumors as well as in
            undifferentiated, proliferative cells together with its inhibitory
            effects on proliferation and G1/S phase transition suggest SELENOH
            as a key regulator for cell cycle progression and for prevention of
            uncontrolled proliferation
REFERENCE   3  (bases 1 to 1192)
  AUTHORS   Barage,S.H., Deobagkar,D.D. and Baladhye,V.B.
  TITLE     Characterization of structural and functional role of
            selenocysteine in selenoprotein H and its impact on DNA binding
  JOURNAL   Amino Acids 50 (5), 593-607 (2018)
   PUBMED   29480333
  REMARK    GeneRIF: the obtained results clearly illustrate Sec residue plays
            an important role to restore functionally active conformation of
            SELENOH. The present study broadened our current understanding
            regarding the role of selenocysteine in protein structure and
            function.
REFERENCE   4  (bases 1 to 1192)
  AUTHORS   Cox,A.G., Tsomides,A., Kim,A.J., Saunders,D., Hwang,K.L.,
            Evason,K.J., Heidel,J., Brown,K.K., Yuan,M., Lien,E.C., Lee,B.C.,
            Nissim,S., Dickinson,B., Chhangawala,S., Chang,C.J., Asara,J.M.,
            Houvras,Y., Gladyshev,V.N. and Goessling,W.
  TITLE     Selenoprotein H is an essential regulator of redox homeostasis that
            cooperates with p53 in development and tumorigenesis
  JOURNAL   Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A 113 (38), E5562-E5571 (2016)
   PUBMED   27588899
  REMARK    GeneRIF: Transcriptome analysis revealed that SepH deficiency
            induces an inflammatory response and activates the p53 pathway.
            Consequently, loss of seph renders larvae susceptible to oxidative
            stress and DNA damage. Finally, we demonstrate that seph interacts
            with p53 deficiency in adulthood to accelerate gastrointestinal
            tumor development
